  :adr/id "adr-2809070200-cleanup-land-walks-worktrees-as-repos"
  :adr/title "cleanup-land walks 96 linked worktrees as repositories and its slug collision skips the real repo; and a third custody plane the search-before-absent rule does not name"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-09-07"
  :adr/context "A `git cleanup conflict` pass on 2026-09-06/07 against com-junkawasaki/root and its
4,514 west checkouts. The read-only surveys came back healthy in the places this
workspace has learned to watch: `scripts/west-orphan-audit.cljs` reported
true-orphan-git 0 and renamed-UNVERIFIED 0, and `scripts/cleanup.cljs --unlanded`
reported 236 UNLANDED child repos of 4,514 scanned (75 holding untracked work),
with its completion line present so the run is known not to have been killed.

The findings below are not about those surveys. They are about the WRITE side,
`scripts/cleanup-land.cljs`, whose dry-run plan for this fleet was 121 targets.
Nothing in this ADR was reached by reading code alone; each item was measured, and
where a both-directions test was available it was run.

This ADR is an evidence record for a cleanup session. It changes no policy on its
own; it names four defects in one tool, one broken edge that was repaired, and one
methodological gap in an existing repo-wide rule that this session walked into."
  :adr/decision "## 1. cleanup-land enumerates linked worktrees as repos (96 of 121 targets)

Measured 2026-09-06: of the 121 targets in the dry-run plan, **96 have a `.git`
that is a FILE** (a linked-worktree gitdir pointer) and only 25 are ordinary
checkouts. Spot-checked three: `orgs/cloud-itonami/.wave5-2022` is a worktree of
`cloud-itonami-isic-2022` sitting on branch `agent/flagship-item2-2022` with 4
uncommitted changes; `.w5-1040` is a worktree of `cloud-itonami-isic-1040` on
`agent/item2-w5-1040`; `.wt-run` is a worktree of `cloud-itonami-app` with 21.

`--apply` would therefore have opened `:review` PRs proposing OTHER SESSIONS'
in-progress work, which its author deliberately put on an `agent/*` branch,
against the parent repository's DEFAULT branch.

The cause is known and is in this repo's own record. `:gitdir-file-blindspot`
(2026-08-22) found that the enumeration `find orgs -maxdepth 3 -name .git -type d`
could not see checkouts whose `.git` is a file, and `orgs/kotoba-lang/kotoba` was
invisible to the tool while holding 7,494 lines of uncommitted work. The fix was to
drop `-type d`. That was correct for the case it addressed. It also made every
linked worktree in the fleet enumerate as a repository, because a worktree's `.git`
is a file for the same reason a submodule-era gitdir pointer is.

Removing a blind spot widened the aperture onto a population nobody intended to
walk. `own-repo-root?` (`rev-parse --show-toplevel` resolving to the directory
itself) is cited in that section as the real defence against stub `.git` files, and
it does not separate these two cases: a linked worktree IS its own toplevel.

The discriminator that does separate them is `git rev-parse --git-common-dir`: for
an ordinary checkout it resolves inside the directory, for a linked worktree it
resolves into the PARENT repository. That question is not asked today.

## 2. The slug collision resolves backwards, and skips the real repo

When two paths map to one GitHub slug, cleanup-land lets the first claim win and
reports the loser. Measured 2026-09-06, the loser was the real repository in both
observed cases:

- `orgs/network-awai/.hyakka-deploy` (a worktree of app-hyakka) claimed
  `network-awai/app-hyakka`, so **`orgs/network-awai/app-hyakka` was skipped with
  67 landing candidates**. That repo is west-registered at
  `path: orgs/network-awai/app-hyakka`; the worktree appears in west 0 times. It
  was also the top rank-1 hazard in the survey (untracked=16 by `git status`).
- `orgs/net-kotobase/control-plane-legal-candidate` claimed
  `net-kotobase/control-plane`, so **control-plane was skipped with 31 candidates**.
- `orgs/cloud-itonami/.cloud-itonami-app-adopt` claimed `cloud-itonami/app`, so
  `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-app` was skipped with 1.

Dot-prefixed names sort before ordinary ones, so the scratch copy wins the race by
construction rather than by accident. The tie-break should prefer the path west
registers; failing that, the checkout whose `--git-common-dir` is its own.

## 3. The west-registration handoff would register worktrees as west projects

The plan's closing section named 99 of 119 landed repos as lacking a `west.yml`
path and printed a ready-to-run command beginning
`nbb scripts/west-triple-sync.cljs plan --names .cloud-itonami-app-adopt,.w5-0124,
.w5-1040,...`.

Those names are worktree directories. The orphan audit's own classification puts
them under `:worktree-scratch` (53) and `:worktree-of-registered` (28) and says
session debris is never auto-registered. So one tool prints a command whose effect
the other tool's rules forbid. The handoff was added (2026-08-23) precisely because
stopping at pushed-to-GitHub is how a GH-only orphan gets made; it needs the same
worktree filter as items 1 and 2, or it converts a correct warning into a wrong
instruction.

## 4. `:additive` with every cleanup-land gate green is still not safe to merge

`kotoba-lang/amu#824`, `cleanup: land untracked WIP (127 files)`, +4553/-0, opened
by an earlier cleanup pass at 09:27Z on 2026-09-06 and NOT a draft. Every gate
cleanup-land owns was green on it. It was nevertheless BLOCKED: the required check
`test (ubuntu-latest)` failed on all three platforms, roughly two minutes into the
job, at this step:

    Run npx nbb scripts/check-workflows.cljs
    workflow-lint: POSIX shell execution files found:
      docs/amufalsify-grep.sh, scripts/amufalsify-dbg-tmp.sh, ... (12 total)

That gate is the repo enforcing CLAUDE.md's 2026-07-14 rule (sh is prohibited,
write it in nbb) on its own contents.

This is the seventh instance of the argument `:residue-gate`, `:nested-repo-gate`,
`:stale-ignore-gate`, `:unresolved-refs-gate`, `:source-twin-gate` and
`:revert-residue-gate` each refute: absent from the default branch is not the same
as new work, and additive-by-path is not the same as safe-to-merge. What is new
here is the SOURCE of the refusal. The six existing gates all encode knowledge
cleanup-land carries. This one came from the target repository, which cleanup-land
never asks.

The general form: **cleanup-land runs a fixed gate list and never consults the
repo's own required checks.** The cheap remedy is not a seventh gate but an order
of operations: for `:additive`, open the PR, then merge only after the repo's
required checks pass, rather than merging on the classification alone. That also
costs nothing where a repo has no checks.

Composition of #824's 127 files, measured: 12 `*.sh` (all `*-tmp.sh`,
`*-20260906*.sh`, `probe-*.sh`), 104 `amubench-append-<timestamp>.py` /
`amufalsify-append-<timestamp>.py`, 8 probe stdout `.txt` and one `.bak114`, and
**3 files of real content**. The scratch is written into the repo by an amu cron
loop and matches no `.gitignore` entry.

Disposition taken: archived the 380 KB patch, landed the 3 real files as
`kotoba-lang/amu#836` reusing #824's blobs verbatim, closed #824 with the evidence.
Proven both directions on the real pipeline, same gate and same repo, differing
only in whether the `.sh` files are present: `check-workflows` FAILURE on #824,
SUCCESS on #836; all three `test` platforms green on #836.

## 5. Test-run output reaches `:additive` because no gate names it

`orgs/kotoba-lang/torihiki` planned `:additive 487 files` and was the only
`:additive` target in the fleet. The repo tracks 60 files and **0** under
`evidence/`; its `.gitignore` names only `/target /.cpcache /node_modules
/.nbb-deps`. The 487 are 214 `.out`, 108 `.err`, 137 `.md`, 25 Clojure files and a
few others, all under `evidence/`, and the `.out` files contain test stdout
(`Running tests in #{\"test\"}`).

`junk-re` does not cover `.out`/`.err`, and `base-ignore-gate!` correctly reported
`scanned 487, excluded 0` because the base `.gitignore` does not exclude them
either. Both gates worked as written. The content is still run output.

Not landed. A previous pass had already opened draft `torihiki#13` for the same
corpus, which is the correct holding action. Whether an evidence corpus belongs in
that repo is a repo-policy question, not a cleanup question.

## 6. A third custody plane that the search-before-concluding-absent rule does not name

This session concluded, and wrote into an archive index, that the content of the
deleted `gftdcojp/ai-gftd-apex` existed nowhere but 7 dangling worktrees. **That
was wrong.** The search covered `orgs/` (fleet-wide grep for the distinctive
literals `hunyuan3d-vrm` and `murakumo-scene-v1`: 0 hits outside those directories)
and GitHub (`gftdcojp` has 9 repositories, full paginated listing, none apex). It
did not cover `~/repo-archive/`.

`manifest/gftd-retirement.edn` lists apex under
`[:closing :landed :deleted-with-verified-bundles]`, and
`~/repo-archive/gftdcojp-apex-20260906.bundle` (632 KB, written 18:42 that day)
reports `The bundle records a complete history` and contains
`src/gftd/apex/generation_api.cljc` byte-identical at 294bda414862 to the worktree
copy.

The repo-wide rule 'search before you conclude X is absent' names
`scripts/repo-search.cljs`, the concept and surface indexes, and `git ls-files -v`
for sparse-cone gaps. Every one of those answers a question about LIVE content.
Retired content lives in a plane none of them reads, and the register that names
that plane is a single EDN file most searches never open. Three custody planes now
exist -- the working tree, GitHub, and the bundle archive -- and the rule names two.

The corrected finding is narrower and was measured both ways. The 7 worktrees are 4
distinct trees differing in exactly one file, `src/gftd/chat/views.cljc` (7 parallel
agent attempts at it). Against every blob for that path across all bundle refs (13
distinct), `claude-2` IS present and `claude-10` (8471d6d8f5a1), `codex-1`
(8a08897f9798) and `codex-5` (6e1252374282) are NOT. Those three uncommitted
variants are the only content in those directories that exists nowhere else. All
357 files were archived by copy and the archive index entry was corrected in place
rather than left standing.

## 7. A repaired edge, and one silenced but unrepaired

REPAIRED. `orgs/network-awai/cloud-itonami/deps.edn` resolved
`io.github.gftdcojp/cloud-mamori` through `../../gftdcojp/cloud-kyusai`. GitHub
redirects that slug to `cloud-itonami/kyusai`, which west carries at
`orgs/cloud-itonami/kyusai`, so the declaration resolved only on machines predating
the move and no fresh checkout could build a classpath for any `-M:local`
invocation. Judged against the consumer's own `origin/main` (the shared checkout was
70 commits behind, and the audit had classified the edge UNJUDGED for exactly that
reason), not against the drifted tree. The residue directory was at the same commit
as the west pin (a7d7203), clean, with an identical `src/` file set. Retargeted at
the west path via a server-side single-entry commit; PR #609 merged; pin advanced
47a98001 -> 0f07d0aa; checkout synced. The audit's `local-root-UNJUDGED` count went
1 -> 0.

SILENCED BUT NOT REPAIRED. The one remaining blocking edge, `orgs/gftdcojp/apex`
consumed by `orgs/network-awai/net-babiniku`, was cleared by a concurrent session
during this one (`35300e3`, merge `1ee08f9`) by deleting the dead `:extra-paths`
entry. Its commit message states that no code here requires an apex.* namespace.
`orgs/network-awai/net-babiniku/scripts/provider_contract_probe.clj` line 4 still
reads `[gftd.apex.generation-api :as apex]`, unchanged. The namespace is
`gftd.apex.*`, not `apex.*`. So `bb provider-contracts` still cannot resolve, and
the audit now exits 0. The retirement claim in that message is correct; the
namespace claim is not."
  :adr/consequences "WHAT IS TRUE NOW (verified at close, 2026-09-07):

- superproject: 0 ahead / 0 behind origin/main, 0 tracked changes, 0 stashes,
  0 open PRs. The one stash (canvas-ledger WIP) was verified landed -- its 3 events
  byte-identical to origin/main lines 10628 and 10630, with an altered-key control
  that correctly missed -- archived, then dropped.
- `nbb scripts/west-orphan-audit.cljs --blocking` exits 0: no blocking registration
  gaps and no un-adjudicated candidates. Note that exit 0 here is partly the result
  of item 7's silencing, not only of item 7's repair.
- non-draft cleanup PRs fleet-wide: 3 -> 0. Every cleanup PR that could merge
  without a human is disposed. `amu#824` closed after rescue; `kotoba-lang#570` and
  `#572` closed as superseded (all 16 and 7 net-added `jvm-dep-ledger.edn` lines
  already on main by semantic key with `:event/at` stripped, against a 193-line
  file, with a control line correctly absent). All three archived before closing.
- merged this session: network-awai/cloud-itonami#609, kotoba-lang/amu#836.
  Pins advanced for both (cloud-itonami -> 0f07d0aa, amu -> b0dcfe4d), each through
  `scripts/west-pin-put.cljs` with its three server-side checks passing.
- archive: `.git/stash-archive-20260906/` holds 4.5 MB -- 3 PR patches, the stash
  patch, and 357 copied files under `orphaned-worktrees/` -- with an index.txt
  entry per item.

WHAT WAS DELIBERATELY NOT DONE:

`cleanup-land --apply` was NOT run, on the evidence of items 1-3. Running it in the
current state would open roughly 90 `:review` PRs, most of them proposing other
sessions' feature-branch work against default branches, and would merge item 5's
487 files of test output.

RESUME POINT, in priority order:

1. Fix cleanup-land's target enumeration before the next `--apply`
   (`scripts/cleanup-land.cljs`). One question separates the two populations:
   `git rev-parse --git-common-dir` resolving outside the directory means linked
   worktree. Gate items 1, 2 and 3 on it, and print the count walked and the count
   skipped as worktrees, so that skipping is distinguishable from finding nothing.
   Prove it both ways as the existing gates do: a real repo still plans, a worktree
   does not, and app-hyakka's 67 candidates become reachable again.
2. Then re-run `nbb scripts/cleanup-land.cljs` and re-read the plan. It should
   shrink from 121 targets to about 25.
3. For `:additive`, merge on the repo's required checks rather than on the
   classification (item 4).
4. Decide torihiki's evidence policy (item 5) -- `torihiki#13` holds it correctly
   meanwhile. Same question for amu's cron scratch, which will re-plan every pass.
5. Repair `net-babiniku`'s `:provider-probe` for real (item 7): either restore
   `gftd.apex.generation-api` from
   `~/repo-archive/gftdcojp-apex-20260906.bundle` into a repo the taxonomy accepts,
   or remove the require and the assertions that depend on it. Do not recreate
   `gftdcojp/apex`: its deletion is recorded and bundle-verified, and recreating it
   would reverse a decision rather than repair a break.
6. Consider naming `~/repo-archive/` and `manifest/gftd-retirement.edn` in the
   search-before-concluding-absent rule (item 6), so the retired plane is reachable
   from the same habit as the live one.

BACKLOG NOT DRAINED, and honestly: fleet open PRs went 103 -> 108 across this
session. 3 were closed and 2 merged here; concurrent loops opened about 8. Of the
108: 31 sit in archived repositories and can be closed by nobody (both verified
archived=true), 25 are cleanup-shaped and all drafts, 52 are ordinary. One pass
does not out-drain the generators, and reporting the backlog as shrinking would be
false."
